	<item>
		<title>NASA Invites Media, Public to View Roman Telescope’s First Images</title>
		<link>https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-invites-media-public-to-view-roman-telescopes-first-images/</link>

		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Jessica Taveau]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 17:00:29 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Goddard Space Flight Center]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Science Mission Directorate]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.nasa.gov/?post_type=press-release&#038;p=1065857</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[NASA will release the first images from its Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope during a live stream from the American Geophysical Union (AGU) Annual Meeting in San Francisco at 3:30 p.m. EST (12:30 p.m. PST) Monday, Dec. 7. The images will showcase the unique scientific capabilities of Roman, which will soon capture deep, panoramic, never-before-seen [&#8230;]]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<div id="" class="hds-media hds-module wp-block-image"><div class="margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline"><div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit "><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="2048" height="1365" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?w=2048" class="attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048" alt="" style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 63% 38%; object-position: 63% 38%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="eager" parallax_attr="" parallax_off="1" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=768,512 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=1024,683 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=1536,1024 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=400,267 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=600,400 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=900,600 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=1200,800 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/54963457618-23b91802ea-k-1.jpg?resize=2000,1333 2000w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px" /></a></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">NASA’s Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, seen here in the clean room at the agency’s Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland, on Nov. 25, 2025, will explore essential questions in the areas of dark energy, exoplanets, and astrophysics.</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit: NASA/Jolearra Tshiteya</div></figcaption></div></div></div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">NASA will release the first images from its Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope during a live stream from the American Geophysical Union (AGU) Annual Meeting in San Francisco at 3:30 p.m. EST (12:30 p.m. PST) Monday, Dec. 7.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The images will showcase the unique scientific capabilities of Roman, which will soon capture deep, panoramic, never-before-seen views of the universe to explore the mysteries of dark energy, dark matter, and worlds outside our solar system.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Watch the unveiling on a variety of platforms online:</p>



<p class="has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/live">https://www.nasa.gov/live</a></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Roman’s first images will simultaneously post on the agency’s <a href="http://www.nasa.gov/RomanFirstImages">website</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The following is a list of activities for release of the first images (all times Eastern):</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Dec. 7</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>12 p.m.:</strong> NASA will preview the images for media under embargo as part of the AGU meeting in San Francisco. To participate, either remotely or in-person, media must be registered with AGU by 8 p.m. Sunday, Dec. 6. Media can apply for AGU registration <a href="https://www.agu.org/annual-meeting/press" rel="noopener">online</a>.<br><br>NASA will update this advisory later with briefing participants.<br><br>The agency also will offer embargoed interviews following the conclusion of the briefing to registered media. A recording of the media briefing will be available on the AGU website after the embargo lifts at 3:30 p.m. on Dec. 7.</li>
</ul>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>2:45 p.m.: </strong>NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman will participate in a <a href="https://www.agu.org/annual-meeting/schedule?tab=keynotes&amp;mkt_tok=OTg3LUlHVC01NzIAAAGlhJUXsgOmQwNwzb4198VCX7sV1greAi7aoLy7dZfswY3AO9EltgMoFSHQ_6fz93wk2mqgNYNY-pHdhZJ26uk" rel="noopener">fireside chat</a> at the AGU meeting, moderated by journalist Alex Witze, to discuss the powerful connections between Earth and space science, the discoveries shaping our understanding of the universe, and the role NASA science plays in advancing what is possible.</li>
</ul>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>3:30 p.m.:</strong> The live broadcast for the release of Roman’s images will stream through a variety of NASA platforms, in addition to AGU channels.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Tuesday, Dec. 8</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>6 a.m. – 1 p.m.:</strong> Roman mission experts available to conduct live, remote interviews with broadcast media. More details about scheduling these interviews are forthcoming.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Pixel adoption</strong><strong></strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">NASA invites the public to <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/roman-space-telescope/adopt-a-pixel/" rel="noopener">claim a corner of the universe</a> with one of Roman’s select first images<strong>. </strong>When Roman reveals its first images, each pixel will represent a unique piece of the cosmos. Once the image is revealed, participants can look up their personal pixel and receive a digital trading card with key information they can share with NASA social accounts.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Roman community events</strong><strong></strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Get involved by <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/roman-space-telescope/events/" rel="noopener">hosting your own event</a> for the reveal of Roman’s first images.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Roman <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-dark-universe-seeking-nancy-grace-roman-space-telescope-launches/">launched</a> Aug. 30 from NASA’s Kennedy Space Center in Florida on a SpaceX Falcon Heavy rocket, beginning a three-month, million-mile trip to its final orbit at the Sun-Earth Lagrange Point 2. During that journey, Roman has undergone a <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/roman-space-telescope/roman-commissioning/" rel="noopener">commissioning process</a> where its systems are turned on, adjusted, calibrated, and prepared to conduct science. Roman will use its large field of view and crisp infrared vision to study vast swaths of the sky and probe deeply into cosmic history. In addition to helping scientists explore dark matter, dark energy, and exoplanets, Roman’s surveys will support a broad range of research extending far beyond the mission’s main science goals. The mission also will feature a cutting-edge coronagraph technology demonstration to directly image planets around other stars.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope is managed at NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland, with participation by NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Southern California; Caltech/IPAC in Pasadena, California; the Space Telescope Science Institute; and a science team comprised of scientists from various research institutions. The primary industrial partners are BAE Systems, Inc.; L3Harris Technologies; and Teledyne Scientific &amp; Imaging.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Stay connected with the mission and share your experience with Roman’s first images on social media. Follow and tag these accounts:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>X: <a href="https://x.com/NASARoman">@NASARoman</a></li>



<li>Facebook: <a href="https://www.facebook.com/NASARoman/" rel="noopener">NASARoman</a></li>



<li>Instagram: <a href="https://www.instagram.com/nasauniverse/" rel="noopener">NASAUniverse</a></li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To learn more about the Roman mission, visit:</p>



<p class="has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/roman"><strong>https://www.nasa.gov/roman</strong></a></p>



<p class="has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph">-end-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Alise Fisher<br>Headquarters, Washington<br>202-617-4977<br><a href="mailto:alise.m.fisher@nasa.gov">alise.m.fisher@nasa.gov</a></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Claire Andreoli<br>Goddard Space Flight Center, Greenbelt, Md.<br>301-286-1940<br><a href="mailto:claire.andreoli@nasa.gov">claire.andreoli@nasa.gov</a></p>


