<h1 class="display-48 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3">Milky Way versus Aurora</h1>
<div class="grid-row media-detail-hero__content-row">
<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6">
<p class="p-md margin-0 media-detail-hero__description">
							<strong>Explanation:</strong> Are these two giant arches battling for control of the sky? No, but it sure looks like it! The arch on the left is the central band of our <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/" rel="noopener">Milky Way Galaxy</a>, an ancient system of stars, gas, and dust that predates <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/planet-earth-through-the-ages/" rel="noopener">our Earth</a> and is very far away. The arch on the right, by contrast, is an <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2004-november-12-missouris-green-ribbon-sky/" rel="noopener">auroral ribbon</a> that is only hours old, composed of energized <a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/articles/zc86m39#zygjdp3" rel="noopener">atoms and molecules</a>, and is close by — right in <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/properties-of-earths-upper-atmosphere/" rel="noopener">Earth’s upper atmosphere</a>. These chance-superposed <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-30-the-surface-of-titan-from-huygens/" rel="noopener">titan</a>s have <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-june-15-prominences-and-filaments-on-the-active-sun/" rel="noopener">our Sun</a> in common, a <a href="https://www.reddit.com/media?url=https%3A%2F%2Fpreview.redd.it%2Fthis-is-the-most-cute-thing-i-ever-seen-v0-mpx697lakcue1.jpg%3Fwidth%3D1080%26crop%3Dsmart%26auto%3Dwebp%26s%3Da5d18b145135737912b5caa61fcc56766726f6cb" rel="noopener">stellar member</a> of the Milky Way that ejects energetic particles which create <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-10-in-green-company-aurora-over-norway/" rel="noopener">aurora</a>s. Far behind the middle of the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-january-13-arches-across-an-arctic-sky/" rel="noopener">auroral arch</a> is the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-april-21-the-big-dipper/" rel="noopener">Big Dipper</a>. Even farther in the distance is the <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/11675/" rel="noopener">Andromeda galaxy</a> — the bright spot near the top of the frame, just left of center. The <a href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DfAZEgmIDIq/" rel="noopener">featured image</a> was taken about a month ago from <a href="https://youtu.be/4VlzJh3WBGU" rel="noopener">Kvaløya</a> in northern <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway" rel="noopener">Norway</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Tomorrow’s picture: </strong>open space						</p>
